{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "128",
    "case_summary": "A 46-year-old woman presented with right flank pain, dysuria, and hematuria. Her past medical history was notable for an abdominal hysterectomy 15 years earlier. General and pelvic examinations were unremarkable. Urinalysis showed 6–8 red blood cells and 4–6 pus cells per high-power field but no other abnormalities.\n\nAn ultrasound of the abdomen and pelvis revealed a focal hypoechoic polypoidal lesion measuring 3.4 × 2.8 cm along the anterosuperior wall of the urinary bladder, containing tiny hyperechoic foci; the outline of the adjacent bladder wall appeared indistinct. The uterus was not visualized; a cystic area was noted in the left adnexa, and both ovaries were not seen.\n\nContrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen and pelvis showed a well-defined soft-tissue–density lesion with calcific specks along the anterosuperior bladder wall that demonstrated heterogeneous enhancement in the early arterial and venous phases. The intraluminal component merged with the bladder wall, causing minimal localized wall thickening; the rest of the bladder wall was normal. Additional findings included a right calyceal calculus with mild pelvicalyceal dilatation, a simple right renal cyst, no pelvic lymphadenopathy, and left hydrosalpinx.\n\nOn imaging, possibilities such as endometriosis, a mucosal neoplastic lesion, or a granulomatous lesion of the urinary bladder were considered.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Endosalpingiosis\nB. endometriosis\nC. mucosal neoplastic lesion\nD. granulomatous lesion\nE. Exclusion of carcinoma on histopathology\nF. Exclusion of endometriosis on histopathology due to lack of endometrial components\nG. Awareness of other mimics requiring histopathologic differentiation\nH. None\n",
